Target behavior
Navigation and panel
The sidebar shows the existing Shift entry. No separate global Switch Location entry appears in the primary navigation.
Opening Shift shows the existing shift controls plus a Location button or tab. The button is available only when the authenticated user is allowed to view or operate a branch.
The Location pane loads the authorized branch directory. Each row shows branch name, active/open status, terminal occupancy, current cashier or operator safe identity, and available actions.
The pane labels the current operating branch separately from the selected viewing branch. A clear "Read-only branch view" banner appears whenever branchViewMode is active.
Leaving branch view returns to the prior POS view and preserves the operating shift, cart, parked-sale state, terminal, and operator session.
Branch directory and cashier status
Show all active branches only to a user with tenant-wide branch-view authority. For a user with scoped authority, show only active locations granted to that user.
For each registered active terminal, show Available, In use, Stale for recovery review, or Unavailable. Include the terminal label and safe cashier/operator display name only.
Show the shift owner and current operator separately when operator sessions exist. Do not use current operator data to rewrite cashier_id or shift ownership.
If a branch has no open shift on any eligible terminal, show "No active shift" and allow Open Shift only when the user has the relevant operating authority.
If one terminal is occupied but another eligible terminal is free, show the free terminal as an opening option. If no eligible terminal is free, show View Branch only and the reason.
View Branch
The user clicks View Branch on a permitted branch row.
The client stores branchViewLocationId and enters branchViewMode without changing operatingLocationId, activeTerminalId, activeShiftId, cart, parked sale, or operator session.
The client requests read-only branch data with an explicit target location. The server validates the user grant or tenant-wide view authority for every request.
The viewer may inspect location-scoped catalog/item availability, reports/history read surfaces, incoming order status, and shift/terminal monitoring only where the user already has the corresponding read permission.
The viewer cannot add items to a sell cart, checkout, pay, split tender, park/resume/cancel a sale, claim a parked sale, change quantities or prices in an active sale, record cash, close or switch another shift, assign operators, edit items, edit settings, modify vouchers/pricelists/affiliates, or mutate orders.
If a route does not have a safe read-only location scope, the UI must show "Not available in branch view" rather than silently falling back to the viewer's operating branch.
Open Branch and Open Shift
The user selects Open Shift from a branch row, not View Branch.
The server rechecks authenticated tenant membership, active user state, pos:transact, target location grant, terminal registry eligibility, strict terminal binding, attendance/break rules, and whether the user already owns an open shift or active operator scope elsewhere.
If the user owns an open shift elsewhere, return a stable conflict such as POS_OPERATOR_ACTIVE_ELSEWHERE. The UI offers View Branch and a link to resume the existing shift; it does not close, move, or transfer it.
If the selected terminal is occupied, return TERMINAL_SHIFT_OCCUPIED with safe owner/status data. No takeover, cash transfer, auto-close, or drawer reassignment occurs.
If the target terminal is free and all checks pass, continue through the existing normal opening flow for opening float and note. The resulting shift is owned by the authenticated opener and receives the target location anchor.
After a successful open, exit branch view, set the new operating context from the server response, refresh shift/catalog/queue/dashboard state, and leave no stale data from the prior branch.
Permission and scope model
Add a dedicated POS read capability, preferably pos:branch_view, to distinguish "may inspect an authorized branch" from pos:switch_location, which remains a register shift move. Keep pos:transact as the authority to open a shift and perform checkout. Tenant-wide branch visibility must come from an explicit master-admin/administrator or existing business-admin all-branches authority, not from a role label alone.Actor or capability
