Update the backend permission catalog and frontend fallback mirror together; the frontend live source remains the server role catalog.
Do not silently grant pos:branch_view to all cashiers. If product wants cashier viewing, make it an explicit grant and test the default denial.
Do not rely on the current tenantLocations.js reference to PERMISSIONS.BUSINESS_ADMIN unless the backend permission catalog is first reconciled; the checked StagingPOS permissions.js currently exposes no BUSINESS_ADMIN group while the frontend mirror and shared capabilities do. This is a confirmed permission-catalog inconsistency and must be resolved in the implementation work package.
Backend and API plan
Branch directory read model
Add one read use case and endpoint rather than making the client call each branch independently. A suitable contract is GET /pos/branch-directory or GET /pos/locations/overview. The endpoint returns only locations in the caller scope and a bounded terminal occupancy summary.Response field
Purpose
Source / rule
location_id, location_name, is_active, is_openBranch identity and statusTenantLocation; active locations onlyaccess_state, access_reasonExplain granted, denied, inactive, or unavailableUserLocationGrant plus tenant-wide authorityterminals[]Eligible terminal listTerminal registry and strict-device policyterminal_id, label, statusAvailable, occupied, stale, unavailableRegistry plus open shift stateshift_id, shift_ownerOccupied shift displayPosTerminalShift.cashier_id; safe display fields onlycurrent_operatorCurrent operator display when lifecycle is enabledPosTerminalOperatorSession; never replaces shift ownercan_view, can_open, can_switchServer-derived actions for this user and rowEffective permissions and scope; UI is not authorityreason_codeStable client behaviorExamples: NO_VIEW_PERMISSION, LOCATION_NOT_GRANTED, SHIFT_OCCUPIED, ACTIVE_ELSEWHERE, TERMINAL_UNAVAILABLE
Read-only branch context
Extend or replace the current admin location monitor use case with a dedicated read-only use case. Do not make read-only viewing depend on pos:switch_location.
Require an explicit location_id on branch-view requests. Resolve it through the same tenant and location-scope helpers used by existing POS reads.
Add a request context such as view_mode=branch_view only for read routes. Mutation use cases must ignore or reject this mode and continue to require the active operating shift/operator/terminal authority.
Inventory catalog, item stock, reports, history, incoming-order, settings, voucher, pricelist, affiliate, audit, and parked-sale endpoints. Mark each as branch-view allowed, current-operating-only, or forbidden. Do not expose mutation-capable forms in the viewer shell.
Use pagination and bounded result limits for branch directory, orders, history, and reports. Avoid one request per branch and avoid returning full transaction or customer payloads merely to show occupancy.
Open shift and conflict contract
Reuse POST /pos/terminal/shifts/open after adding explicit stable conflict codes and ensuring the transaction locks the authenticated user, target terminal, and relevant open-shift rows.
Preserve same-terminal resume behavior. A retry must return the existing owned shift, not create a second row.
Return 409 for the authenticated user already operating elsewhere or the target terminal already occupied; do not expose raw database errors.
Keep POST /pos/terminal/shifts/:id/switch-location as the governed close-plus-open operation for its existing use case. It is not the View Branch path.
Ensure strict device terminal policies remain binding. A viewer may inspect a strict terminal, but cannot use branch view to bypass device pairing or change the terminal home location.
Audit and security
Audit branch-view entry and exit as VIEW events with actor, tenant, viewed location, operating location, operating terminal, active shift ID if any, request ID, and timestamp. Do not store passwords, tokens, full cart snapshots, or unnecessary customer data.
Audit denied branch-view attempts and occupied/open conflicts at a bounded rate or through existing security/audit conventions; include reason_code, not secrets.
Audit normal shift opening through its existing shift audit path. Do not create financial or drawer evidence for View Branch.
The branch directory must use safe cashier/operator identity fields. Never return credentials, PIN state, access tokens, or private account data.
Re-read the current tenant user and effective permissions on protected mutations. Cached frontend permission state may only control display, never authorization.
On permission revocation, location grant removal, user deactivation, or shift closure, the next branch-view refresh must fail closed or remove the row. The viewer must not retain stale action buttons.
