Verification strategy
Backend tests
Permission tests: default cashier denied; explicit branch-view grant allowed; manager/admin/master-admin scope behavior; pos:switch_location does not implicitly grant read-only view unless explicitly defined.
Tenant and grant tests: cross-tenant location IDs return generic 403/404 as appropriate; unassigned locations are not listed or readable; inactive branches are not openable.
Occupancy tests: occupied terminal returns a stable conflict; viewer can still read status; another user cannot close, switch, cash-adjust, claim parked sales, or mutate the occupied shift.
One-active-scope concurrency: two opens for one user yield one durable shift/operator authority; the losing request returns a conflict; no auto-close, transfer, or extra financial rows.
Read-only mutation tests: branch-view requests cannot execute checkout, payment, parked-sale, cash, shift, item, settings, order, voucher, pricelist, affiliate, or audit mutations.
Operator attribution tests: shift owner and current operator remain separate; viewer identity is never written as cashier_id or operator session.
Frontend and browser tests
The sidebar has no standalone Switch Location entry; Shift contains the Location action.
Branch rows show safe cashier/terminal status and correct buttons for free, occupied, inactive, unauthorized, and unavailable states.
View Branch leaves the active shift, terminal, cart, parked sale, and operating branch unchanged; entering and exiting view refreshes data without stale branch responses.
Viewer mode hides or disables mutation controls and displays a persistent read-only banner on desktop and mobile/tablet.
Open Shift from a free branch uses the normal opening-float flow and becomes the new operating context only after the server succeeds.
A user with an existing shift sees View Branch but not a takeover/open action for a different terminal; the original shift remains resumable.
Offline and reconnect: branch directory becomes unavailable or stale with an explicit message; no offline viewer or shift-open action fabricates authority.
Acceptance criteria
A permitted user opens Shift, selects Location, sees the authorized branch directory, and sees who is operating each occupied terminal without seeing credentials or sensitive data.
Clicking View Branch opens the selected POS branch as read-only and never changes the user's active shift, terminal, drawer, cart, parked sale, operator session, or operating branch.
The viewer cannot sell, checkout, pay, park/resume/cancel/claim sales, record cash, close or switch another shift, edit catalog/settings, mutate orders, or alter voucher/pricelist/affiliate/audit data.
A branch with no open shift and a free eligible terminal offers Open Shift only when the user has the required permission, active location grant, terminal eligibility, attendance state, and no existing active operating scope.
A branch or terminal with an existing shift never offers takeover through this feature. The UI shows occupied status and the server returns a stable conflict if a race occurs.
A user with an active shift can view other authorized branches without losing the active shift and can resume the original shift after leaving branch view.
Strict device-terminal rules remain enforced, and no branch-view path bypasses terminal pairing or home-location binding.
All branch-view entry, exit, denial, and open-shift conflict evidence is auditable without storing secrets.
The plan's verification evidence proves desktop and mobile/tablet behavior, tenant isolation, permission scope, concurrent shift safety, and no mutation leakage.
Rollout and rollback
Release behind a tenant capability flag if the existing POS feature-flag pattern supports it. Default off until API, UI, and browser evidence pass.
Roll out read-only branch directory first, then View Branch, then Open Shift. Do not enable shift-location mutation changes as part of this feature.
Monitor 403/409 reason codes, branch-directory latency, read-scope failures, viewer-to-operating transitions, and audit write failures.
Rollback by disabling the feature flag and hiding the Shift Location pane. Preserve all existing shifts, carts, parked sales, operator sessions, and audit history. Do not roll back by deleting data.
If a permission backfill is introduced, its rollback must be an explicit reviewed permission reconciliation; never blindly overwrite stored permission arrays.
Open questions and blockers before implementation
Reconcile the authoritative phase ledger. The checked ledger currently ends at Phase 347, while separate reference documents mention future Phases 348-357. Assign this initiative a new non-conflicting ledger phase only after that discrepancy is resolved; LS-01 to LS-07 are deliberately provisional work-package labels.
Confirm whether "all branches" means all active tenant locations for administrators or all locations granted to the signed-in user. This plan recommends tenant-wide visibility only for explicit administrator/master-admin authority and grant-scoped visibility for everyone else.
Confirm the exact set of read-only modules to expose in branch view. This plan includes POS operational reads where already authorized but intentionally excludes settings, authoring, and financial/order mutations.
Confirm the supported operator-session migration coverage across StagingPOS tenants before showing current operator separately from shift owner.
Resolve the backend PERMISSIONS.BUSINESS_ADMIN reference mismatch before adding or reusing all-branch authorization. Do not build a new route around an undefined backend permission constant.
